        #    One more nuance: In merging target intervals, we used `if s <= cur_e`. Since intervals are closed, this merges touching intervals (e.g., [1,3] and [3,4] -> [1,4]). That is correct because point 3 belongs to both, so they together cover [1,4] continuously. However, is it necessary to merge touching intervals? Consider target intervals [1,3] and [4,5]. They do not overlap (3 < 4), so no merge. If they touch at 3 and 4? That would be [1,3] and [3,4] touch at 3, but not at 4? Actually [1,3] ends at 3, [3,4] starts at 3, they share point 3, so they overlap at that point, thus should be merged into [1,4]. Yes, because the union of [1,3] and [3,4] is [1,4] (closed). So merging with <= is correct.

